Un-stub the v3 UI redesign — Domain J: Comment/issue/review timestamps

## Current state

Confirmed by reading `crates/gix-forge/src/comment.rs`, `issue.rs`, and `review.rs` in full: none of `Comment`, `Issue`, or `Review` (nor their stored counterparts) carry any explicit `created_at`/`updated_at` field. The only time-adjacent field anywhere is `edit: Option<String>` (an edit-note string, not a timestamp). `crates/git-forge-ui/src/pages/issues.rs` already has a doc comment acknowledging this gap explicitly: "since `Comment` itself stores no per-comment signature or timestamp."

But an implicit timestamp already exists one layer down, and it is not surfaced. `crates/gix-forge/src/entity.rs` confirms every entity is stored as a real git commit: `EntityOps::create_in_repo_as`/`save_as` call into `gix-store`'s write path, which bottoms out in a real commit-write with `author`/`committer` set to real `gix::actor::Signature`s carrying wall-clock timestamps, exactly like an ordinary `git commit`. Every create and every update (comments are editable via the CLI's `CommentCommand::Edit`) produces a new commit at the entity's ref, so the ref's history already encodes a full "created → edited → edited" timeline for free. The store's history-listing API returns commit ids tip-first; the oldest entry is creation, the tip is the current version's timestamp. The UI layer even has a ready-made formatter for this shape of data: `format_age()` in `crates/git-forge-ui/src/pages/files.rs`, already used for git commit ages elsewhere in the UI.

What's missing is plumbing, not schema: the entity-loading path carries a commit id alongside the loaded value internally, but `EntityOps::rebuild()` discards it, keeping only the value. No commit's timestamp is ever read back out.

## Target state

Issue/Review/Comment detail views and comment threads show "created N ago" (and "edited N ago" when history length > 1), sourced from the underlying commit signatures — no new stored fields.

## Technical approach

Extend `Entity`/`EntityOps` to preserve provenance through `load`/`load_all`/`entries` (e.g. a `LoadedWith<Self>` or a sibling method returning `(Self, ObjectId)`/`Entry<Self>`), then resolve `created` from `history(id).last()` and `updated` from the tip commit id via `repo.find_commit(oid)?.time()` (author or committer time — author is more meaningful for "created," since a signed committer could differ). This is additive plumbing through an existing internal API, not a schema migration — no schema-versioning/field changes needed, so the schema-versioning/migration pattern used elsewhere in this codebase (confirmed precedented by the commit that added `edit: Option<String>` to all three entities) is not required for this feature. It would only become relevant if product later wants a timestamp independent of git authorship (e.g. imported/backfilled data).

## New dependencies / schema changes

None. This is a plumbing change through existing `gix-forge`/`gix-store` APIs plus UI wiring to the already-existing `format_age` helper — explicitly not a schema migration.

## Open product/UX questions

1. Created-only vs. created+updated — updated is cheap here since it's just the tip commit, but for Issue/Review "updated" conflates body edits with status/label/assignee changes; decide if that's acceptable or if a separate "activity" signal is wanted later.
2. Author time vs. committer time when they diverge (e.g. imported history).
3. Whether to expose full edit history (all commits) in the UI now that it's cheap to reach.

## Complexity: Small

The implicit-git-timestamp angle substantially shrinks this from how it might first appear — no new field, no migration, no backfill-compatibility concern for existing stored comments (their commits already have valid timestamps from when they were written). It's a plumbing change through existing `Entry`/history APIs plus UI wiring.
